---
import { OFFER } from '../../config/offer';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BreadcrumbsEnhanced from '../../components/seo/BreadcrumbsEnhanced.astro';
import FAQEnhanced from '../../components/seo/FAQEnhanced.astro';
import ProductDemo from '../../components/ProductDemo.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Where do the reviews go?",
    answer: "To your Google Business Profile. That is the listing that shows up when someone searches for a driving instructor in your town, so it is the one worth building.",
    links: []
  },
  {
    question: "Who gets asked, and when?",
    answer: "When you mark a pupil's practical test as passed, DriveSchoolPro emails them a request to review you on Google. You choose how long it waits, from straight away up to three days; requests with a wait go out in a daily morning batch. It asks each pupil once, and only if they have an email address. Pupils you have marked inactive also appear in a Review Requests list on your Today screen, so you can ask them with one click.",
    links: []
  },
  {
    question: "Does it send the request for me?",
    answer: "For a practical pass, yes. It is on by default and you can switch it off in settings. If you would rather ask yourself, turn it off and use the Review Requests list on your Today screen instead: each pupil is one click, so you stay in control of who gets asked.",
    links: []
  },
  {
    question: "Can I change the wording?",
    answer: "Not yet. The email is a short, friendly congratulations from your school's name with a button that opens your Google review page, and the wording is fixed. You can change when it is sent, or switch it off and ask in person or by message instead.",
    links: []
  },
  {
    question: "Will this get me ranked higher on Google?",
    answer: "We would not promise that, and be sceptical of anyone who does. What we can say plainly is what the tool does: it sends review requests to your Google Business Profile at the moment a pupil is most likely to respond. What Google does with the result is Google's business.",
    links: []
  },
  {
    question: "What if a pupil leaves a bad review?",
    answer: "Reviews are between the pupil and Google — nothing is filtered or intercepted, and it would be against Google's policies to try. A steady stream of genuine reviews is the best answer to an occasional poor one.",
    links: []
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Automated Google Review Collection for Driving Instructors | DriveSchoolPro"
  description="DriveSchoolPro emails a Google review request to pupils who have just passed their practical test, when they are happiest to write one. Free until 31 March 2027."
>
  <BreadcrumbsEnhanced items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Features', href: '/features/' }, { label: 'Reviews' }]} site="driveschool" />

  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
        <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-400 mb-3">End of month</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-navy leading-tight mb-6">
          The reviews you meant to ask for, actually asked for.
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          Word of mouth got you every pupil you have ever had. It is also the only part of your
          marketing you cannot do more of by trying harder — you cannot make more people bump into
          each other at the school gates. The nearest thing to scaling it is a public trail of
          people saying you were good, and that means reviews you never quite get round to asking
          for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ProductDemo
        poster="/images/marketing/review-requests.webp"
        posterAlt="The Review Requests list on the Today screen: two pupils who have just passed their test, each with a Send Email button to ask for a Google review."
        alt="The Review Requests list on the Today screen: two pupils who have just passed their test, each with a Send Email button to ask for a Google review."
        width={1216}
        height={277}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-gray-50 border-t border-gray-100">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl space-y-14">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-4">Asked on the best day of their year</h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-4">
          Timing does almost all the work here. A learner who has just passed is, for about
          forty-eight hours, delighted with you and telling everyone. That is when a request lands
          well and gets a warm, specific, genuinely useful review.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
          Three weeks later they have a car, a job to get to and no particular feelings about
          driving lessons. Same pupil, same experience, no review. Automating the ask is really
          just a way of never missing that window.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-4">It asks so you don't have to remember</h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
          Almost every instructor knows they should ask for reviews. Very few do it consistently,
          and not through laziness — asking requires remembering, at the exact right moment, while
          driving to the next pickup. DriveSchoolPro does that half. Mark a pupil's
          practical test as passed and a short email goes out asking them to review you on Google,
          once, after a wait you choose. Anyone you have marked inactive shows up in a Review
          Requests list on your Today screen, ready for you to ask with one click. Over a year that
          is the difference between four reviews and forty.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-4">Straight to your Google profile</h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
          Requests point at your Google Business Profile — the listing people actually see when they
          search for an instructor in your town — and the email comes from your school. We will not
          promise you a ranking; nobody honestly can. What the tool does is narrow and useful: it
          asks the right person at the right moment, so the ask stops depending on
          you remembering.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white border-t border-gray-100">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-8">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="space-y-6">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-brand-red text-white font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy mb-1">Connect your Google Business Profile</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600">From settings. Without it the feature stays hidden, since there would be nowhere to send anyone.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-brand-red text-white font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy mb-1">Choose when it goes out</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600">From immediately to three days after the pass. It is on by default and you can switch it off.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-brand-red text-white font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy mb-1">Mark the pass, or ask from Today</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600">Marking a practical pass sends the request automatically. Pupils you have marked inactive appear in the Review Requests list on your Today screen, one click each.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQEnhanced faqs={faqs} site="driveschool" pageUrl="https://driveschoolpro.com/features/reviews/" title="Questions about review collection" />

  <section class="section bg-brand-navy text-white">
    <div class="container-custom text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Let your finished pupils find you the next ones</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-300 mb-8">Free until 31 March 2027. No card, no contract, cancel any time.</p>
      <a href={OFFER.ctaPrimary.href} class="btn-primary text-lg px-8 py-4">{OFFER.ctaPrimary.label} &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white border-t border-gray-100">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">Related</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-lg">
        <li><a href="/features/getting-paid/" class="text-brand-red font-semibold hover:underline">Getting paid &rarr;</a> <span class="text-gray-500">— the pupils who finish are the ones who paid up front</span></li>
        <li><a href="/features/progress-tracking/" class="text-brand-red font-semibold hover:underline">Progress tracking &rarr;</a> <span class="text-gray-500">— getting them test-ready in the first place</span></li>
        <li><a href="/blog/seo-for-driving-schools/" class="text-brand-red font-semibold hover:underline">SEO for driving schools &rarr;</a> <span class="text-gray-500">— the wider picture on getting found</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
